# Welcome!

Welcome to the course!

- **Course 1** is an introduction to Web programming, including:
  - HTML fundamentals (module 1)
  - CSS fundamentals (module 2)
  - Programming fundamentals in JavaScript (Module 3)
  - JavaScript basics (module 4)
- **Course 2** goes deeper into JavaScript
- **Course 3** goes further
- **Course 4** focuses on frameworks

There are a combination of (1) readings like this, (2) videos, and (3) exercises. Beyond the readings and videos, **the exercises are crucial to your learning**.

## What Will You Learn?

The focus of this specialization is on building _interactive_ web applications. By "interactive", we mean applications that are responsive to user input (e.g., mouse clicks, keyboard input, etc.). We will learn _just enough_ HTML and CSS to get started but the focus will be on **JavaScript**.

### Why JavaScript?

JavaScript is the language of the web and [the most widely used programming language](https://survey.stackoverflow.co/2025/technology#most-popular-technologies-language-language). It powers the interactive elements of virtually every modern website. JavaScript can power web applications, mobile applications, desktop applications, and even server-side applications. It is a versatile language that can be used for a wide range of applications.

## How to Use These Notebooks

- If you want to load these on your own computer, you can (optionally):
  1. Install [Visual Studio Code](https://code.visualstudio.com/)
  2. Install [the WebNB extension](https://marketplace.visualstudio.com/items/?itemName=oney.web-notebook)
  3. Download [the course repository from GitHub](https://github.com/soney/Practical-JavaScript)
- However, you can also use the web version of the notebooks. You can run them in your browser without installing anything.

These notebooks come equipped with interactive exercises. For example, in the following code cell, try to:

1. Change the number `1` to `5` so that it reads:
   ```javascript
   const x = 5;
   ```
2. Click the "Run" button to run the code cell.
3. Look at the output below the code cell, which should confirm that you completed the exercise correctly.

```{javascript}
const x = 1;
```

```+solution
const x = 5;
```

```+test
wrap(x).should('equal', 5).run('Failed; x should be 5', 'Nice job!');
```

There are also multiple choice questions like the following:

```{mcq}
What will we be learning in this course? (Select all that apply)
correct: Correct. We will be learning HTML, CSS, and JavaScript.
wrong: Try again. Review the question and choose the options that match the lesson.
[x] HTML
[x] CSS
[x] JavaScript
[ ] Python
[ ] Java
```

